{
  "regeln": [
    "Center scripts and concluding scenes strictly on core leadership conflicts, equity splits, and control transitions, eliminating commercial sales milestones and product trivia.",
    "Match visuals directly to the specific business action narrated—such as contracts, capital funding, corporate restructurings, or boardroom votes—rather than relying on generic manual labor or vague period filler.",
    "Depict historical participants using era-appropriate role archetypes without reproducing real-life facial likenesses, living portraits, or commercial brand logos.",
    "Feature distinct visual settings and staged interactions for competing executives, investors, and founders to clearly reflect shifting power dynamics.",
    "Emphasize high-stakes financial crises, near-bankruptcy events, and structural obstacles to preserve narrative tension rather than portraying smooth corporate growth.",
    "Introduce a compelling visual hook in the opening seconds that immediately establishes the primary corporate conflict and bridges into subsequent scenes.",
    "Verify all corporate dates, founder roles, executive titles, and legal transitions against source records before framing dramatic turns.",
    "Align environmental details, architecture, machinery, and wardrobe precisely with the specific historical era and setting mentioned in the script.",
    "Adapt narrative depth and visual rhythm strictly to the chosen format, keeping Shorts tightly focused on rapid conflict beats while allowing deliberate scene progression in long-form videos."
